WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.890
Three of my students

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.630
measured the same desk.

3
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.580
Kemi wrote seventy.

4
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.660
Tunde wrote seven hundred.

5
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.240
Grace wrote twenty-eight.

6
00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:16.990
Funny thing is, they could all be right.

7
00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.610
Today: measure it right, write it down

8
00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:24.470
well, and catch readings that can't be

9
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:24.790
true.

10
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.070
Look at their tapes.

11
00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.430
Kemi measured seventy centimetres.

12
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.800
Tunde measured seven hundred

13
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.680
millimetres: the same length.

14
00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.310
Grace used a tailor's tape in

15
00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:39.130
inches: about twenty-eight.

16
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.580
A number without its unit

17
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.140
means almost nothing.

18
00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:45.870
Next, read the scale.

19
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.190
Find two numbered marks, and count

20
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.630
the small gaps between them.

21
00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.370
Ten gaps from twenty to thirty?

22
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:55.890
Each gap is worth one.

23
00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.470
Four gaps from zero to twenty?

24
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.360
Each is worth five.

25
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.820
Then bring your eye level with the mark.

26
00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.510
Look from above or below, and

27
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.170
you'll line up the wrong mark.

28
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.410
With water in a tube, read at the

29
00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:13.130
bottom of its curved surface.

30
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:14.920
Quick check!

31
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.660
My measuring cylinder has five small gaps

32
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.820
between ten and twenty millilitres.

33
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.980
The water is three gaps above ten.

34
00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.240
What's the reading?

35
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:27.860
Sixteen millilitres.

36
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.980
Ten millilitres across five gaps

37
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.020
makes each gap worth two.

38
00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.470
Three gaps is six, and ten

39
00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:37.770
plus six is sixteen.

40
00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.690
Thirteen is the trap: it

41
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.490
counts every gap as one.

42
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:44.810
Now write it down.

43
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.510
A good record has four parts:

44
00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.910
number, unit, time and place.

45
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.320
Thirty-one degrees Celsius, two in the

46
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.240
afternoon, back of the classroom.

47
00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.450
Without a time and place,

48
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.310
nobody can compare it later.

49
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.100
Sometimes a reading can't be right.

50
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.100
Here's a week of classroom

51
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.780
temperatures: twenty-eight,

52
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.480
twenty-nine, then eighty-five!

53
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.800
No classroom gets that hot.

54
00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.880
With it, the week's

55
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.420
average is forty degrees.

56
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.200
Without it, about twenty-nine.

57
00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.600
A reading like that is a glitch: maybe a

58
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.000
loose wire, or a slip of the pen.

59
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.540
Don't rub it out, and don't just trust it.

60
00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:33.070
Measure again, or check with another tool.

61
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.800
Then mark it, keep it, and

62
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:36.980
note why you left it out.

63
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:38.740
Your turn.

64
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.810
The garden's soil moisture sensor reads

65
00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.950
fifty percent, then exactly zero at

66
00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.550
midnight, then fifty again an hour later.

67
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.830
What do you do with that zero?

68
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.460
Mark it, keep it and check the

69
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.200
wires: soil doesn't dry out and

70
00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.540
get wet again in an hour.

71
00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.240
Remember: the unit, the gap, eye level.

72
00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.220
Number, unit, time and place.

73
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.240
And check readings that can't be right.

74
00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.190
Try it tomorrow: measure your desk

75
00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:15.250
three times, the same way each time.

76
00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.000
Write each reading with its

77
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.340
number, unit, time and place.

78
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.260
Then compare with a friend, and if

79
00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.580
you disagree, find out why.

80
00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:31.350
Next time: connected things, safely.
